---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import hero from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/hs-app-hero.png?url";
import wireframe1 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/wireframe-1.png?url";
import wireframe2 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/wireframe-2.png?url";
import wireframe3 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/wireframe-3.png?url";
import design1 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/design-1.png?url";
import design2 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/design-2.png?url";
import design3 from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/design-3.png?url";
import animation from "../assets/images/work/hs-app/chat-animation.mov?url";
import SectionContainer from "../components/foundation/SectionContainer.astro";
import LargeContainer from "../components/foundation/LargeContainer.astro";
import SmallContainer from "../components/foundation/SmallContainer.astro";
---

<Layout title="ForeFlight Feature First Run Case Study">
	<main>
		<header
			class="bg-[#d5eaf8] pb-32 pt-8 md:px-10 md:pb-32 md:pt-16 lg:pb-56 lg:pt-20"
		>
			<div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-7xl">
				<img
					src={hero}
					alt="Headline Studio Screenshot"
					class="mx-auto w-full pb-12 md:pb-8"
				/>
				<div
					class="grid gap-y-6 px-4 md:gap-x-8 md:px-0 lg:grid-cols-[3fr_1fr] lg:gap-x-16"
				>
					<div class="grid content-start gap-y-2 lg:gap-y-4">
						<h1
							class="text-3xl font-medium leading-normal md:text-4xl md:leading-normal lg:text-5xl"
						>
							ForeFlight Feature Onboarding
						</h1>
						<p
							class="text-sm leading-loose md:text-base md:leading-loose lg:text-lg lg:leading-loose"
						>
							A project summary on the process of designing and building an
							AI-powered feature in the Headline Studio&nbsp;app.
						</p>
					</div>
					<aside
						class="border-t border-solid border-slate-300 pt-6 text-sm leading-loose text-slate-600 lg:justify-self-end lg:border-0 lg:pt-0 lg:text-base"
					>
						<ul class="grid gap-y-2 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-x-4 lg:grid-cols-1">
							<li>
								<span class="font-medium lg:text-base lg:leading-loose"
									>My role</span
								>
								<ul class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2">
									<li
										class="flex items-center gap-x-2 after:inline-block after:h-1 after:w-1 after:rounded-sm after:bg-slate-600"
									>
										Research
									</li>
									<!-- <li
										class="flex items-center gap-x-2 after:inline-block after:h-1 after:w-1 after:rounded-sm after:bg-slate-600"
									>
										Product design
									</li> -->
									<li>Product design</li>
								</ul>
							</li>
							<li>
								<span class="font-medium lg:text-base lg:leading-loose"
									>Tools used</span
								>
								<ul class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2">
									<li
										class="flex items-center gap-x-2 after:inline-block after:h-1 after:w-1 after:rounded-sm after:bg-slate-600"
									>
										Google Docs
									</li>
									<!-- <li
										class="flex items-center gap-x-2 after:inline-block after:h-1 after:w-1 after:rounded-sm after:bg-slate-600"
									>
										Figma
									</li> -->
									<li>Figma</li>
								</ul>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</aside>
				</div>
			</div>
		</header>
		<div class="-mt-20 leading-loose lg:-mt-28">
			<SectionContainer>
				<LargeContainer>
					<div class="bg-white px-4 pb-12 pt-4 md:pb-20 md:pt-10">
						<SmallContainer>
							<div class="grid gap-y-4">
								<p class="lg:text-lg lg:leading-loose">
									For the launch of ForeFlight's new Aeronautical Drawer
									feature, we needed to create an effective onboarding
									experience to guide users through the changes. This was our
									first time implementing a first-run experience, and we sought
									a solution that would improve user understanding while
									minimizing confusion and support&nbsp;inquiries.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">
									About ForeFlight
								</h2>
								<p>
									ForeFlight is a leading aviation app that provides pilots with
									comprehensive flight planning tools, real-time weather
									information, and interactive maps. The app helps pilots
									navigate safely and efficiently, offering an array of
									aeronautical charts and features that are critical for flight
									planning and in-flight&nbsp;navigation.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">
									What problem are we solving?
								</h2>
								<p>
									ForeFlight’s team was introducing a new feature, the
									Aeronautical Drawer, to improve user experience. This feature
									reorganized the aeronautical layer quick filters and settings
									to reduce map clutter, increase real estate, and streamline
									settings management. The challenge was to communicate these
									changes to current users effectively, while reducing confusion
									and minimizing support&nbsp;requests.
								</p>
								<p>
									The goal was to create a first-run experience (a feature that
									ForeFlight had not used before for feature launches) to inform
									users about the updates and help them understand how to use
									the new Aeronautical Drawer. Additionally, it was important
									that the experience be seamless, without disrupting the
									user's&nbsp;workflow.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">Approach</h2>
								<p>
									To craft the first-run experience, we considered
									several&nbsp;approaches.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Research & Inspiration
								</h3>
								<p>
									Inspired by Apple’s “tips” feature and Alejandro’s earlier
									work experimenting with in-app tips, we sought a solution that
									was as clear and simple as&nbsp;possible.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Iteration & Exploration
								</h3>
								<p>
									A key challenge with some of these options was that they
									removed users from the context of the app, which led to a
									disjointed experience. After feedback from pilots and internal
									testing, we refined our&nbsp;approach.
								</p>
								<p>
									Mobile devices were the primary platform, so we designed the
									first-run experience with a small screen in mind. However, we
									encountered additional challenges on the iPad due to the
									larger screen and more spaced-out elements, which made it
									easier for users to miss key&nbsp;information.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									User Context & Conditions
								</h3>
								<p>We took into account specific conditions:</p>
								<ul class="grid list-disc gap-y-2 pl-8">
									<li>
										The user must be connected to the internet and not currently
										flying.
									</li>
									<li>
										Only current customers would see the first-run experience,
										as new users wouldn’t have experienced the previous feature
										setup.
									</li>
									<li>
										Inline tips in the settings menu notified users about the
										move of the Aeronautical Settings.
									</li>
								</ul>
							</div>
						</SmallContainer>
						<LargeContainer>
							<div class="mb-8 mt-16 grid gap-4">
								<img
									src={wireframe1}
									alt="Low fideltiy wireframes"
									class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
								/>
								<div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
									<img
										src={wireframe2}
										alt="Low fideltiy wireframes"
										class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
									/>
									<img
										src={wireframe3}
										alt="Low fideltiy wireframes"
										class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
									/>
								</div>
							</div>
						</LargeContainer>
						<SmallContainer>
							<div class="grid gap-y-4">
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">Solution</h2>
								<!-- <h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">Wireframes</h3>
								<p>
									I delved into various interface options for Headline AI. In
									addition, I explored the different actions a user could take
									with the generated headlines, including copying, editing, and
									analyzing. Ultimately, after careful consideration, our team
									decided that a chat interface was the most user-friendly and
									familiar&nbsp;option.
								</p>
								<p>
									For the MVP, we kept the interface simple with just a button
									to analyze the headline, as additional options would have
									added unnecessary complexity. The chat interface effectively
									communicates a back-and-forth brainstorming tool and provides
									flexibility for future developments of this&nbsp;feature.
								</p> -->
							</div>
						</SmallContainer>
						<LargeContainer>
							<div class="mb-8 mt-16 grid gap-4">
								<img
									src={design1}
									alt="High fidelity design mockups"
									class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
								/>
								<div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
									<img
										src={design2}
										alt="High fidelity design mockups"
										class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
									/>
									<img
										src={design3}
										alt="High fidelity design mockups"
										class="border border-solid border-slate-200"
									/>
								</div>
							</div>
						</LargeContainer>
						<SmallContainer>
							<div class="grid gap-y-4">
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">Design</h3>
								<p>
									Designing the prompt and response experience for Headline AI
									was a critical step in making the feature intuitive and
									engaging. Using Figma, I created an interface that seamlessly
									integrated with the rest of the Headline Studio&nbsp;app.
								</p>
								<p>I focused on:</p>
								<ul class="grid list-disc gap-y-2 pl-8">
									<li>
										Formatting responses that were easily readable and scannable
										for&nbsp;users.
									</li>
									<li>
										Incorporating a Headline Bot persona to communicate with
										users in a fun, conversational, and bot-like&nbsp;language.
									</li>
									<li>
										Error handling, anticipating and designing for parsing
										errors and response&nbsp;timeouts.
									</li>
								</ul>
								<p>
									Headline Studio has free and paid plans. The Headline AI
									feature is only available to paid users. To give free users a
									taste of the Headline AI feature and incentivize them to
									upgrade to the paid plan, we designed the experience to allow
									them to interact with the feature, but are prompted to upgrade
									to unlock the AI response with generated&nbsp;headlines.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Build & Launch
								</h3>
								<p>
									At this phase I was responsible for implementing the HTML &
									CSS for Headline AI. I worked closely with an engineer to
									complete all necessary backend&nbsp;functionality.
								</p>
							</div>
						</SmallContainer>
						<LargeContainer>
							<video
								controls
								autoplay
								muted
								loop
								class="mb-8 mt-16 border border-solid border-slate-200"
							>
								<source src={animation} />
								Your browser does not support the &lt;video&gt; tag.
							</video>
						</LargeContainer>
						<SmallContainer>
							<div class="grid gap-y-4">
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Test & Refine
								</h3>
								<p>
									After the initial launch, we observed a couple of areas that
									needed&nbsp;improvement.
								</p>
								<ul class="grid list-disc gap-y-2 pl-8">
									<li>Initial greeting and prompt animation</li>
									<li>Response time</li>
								</ul>
								<p>
									First, from watching Hotjar I noticed users leaving before the
									chat experience had fully animated in. My assumption was that
									it was not immediately clear to these users what they could do
									with the feature. We wanted to keep the animation because it
									communicated to the user the chat-like experience. However I
									did speed it up so users can see the prompts within less than
									a second of visiting the AI&nbsp;tab.
								</p>
								<p>
									Second, for our Headline AI analytics dashboard noticed
									response times were creeping up, causing users to leave before
									getting a response back. To address these issues, we
									considered using a different loading indicator that was more
									informative and made the wait time feel faster. However, we
									decided to experiment with streaming in responses instead of
									showing everything all at once. This approach improved the
									response time and reduced the likelihood of users leaving
									before getting a full&nbsp;response.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">Impact</h2>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">Engagement</h3>
								<p>
									Most users completed the first-run experience, ensuring they
									understood the changes and how to use the new&nbsp;feature.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									User Group Feedback
								</h3>
								<p>
									Many users in the ForeFlight User Group on Facebook suggested
									we implement this type of experience for other feature
									releases. This positive reception indicated that users
									appreciated the clarity provided by the onboarding&nbsp;flow.
								</p>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Support Reduction
								</h3>
								<p>
									There was a notable reduction in support inquiries related to
									the Aeronautical Drawer, suggesting that the first-run
									experience successfully reduced&nbsp;confusion.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">
									Learnings & Next Steps
								</h2>
								<p>
									During the development process of this feature, I had the
									opportunity to lead ideation and design discussions, which
									helped me improve my communication and leadership&nbsp;skills.
								</p>
								<p>
									In addition, I experimented with prototypes in Figma, which
									helped me deepen my knowledge of the tool. By creating a
									working prototype, I was better able to communicate my ideas
									to the team and receive useful&nbsp;feedback.
								</p>
								<p>
									While working on this project, I found inspiration in the book <span
										class="italic">The Design of Everyday Things</span
									> by Don Norman. Norman emphasizes the importance of designing
									for failure. This concept resonated with me, as I applied it to
									ensure that the Headline AI feature would provide users with a
									seamless experience, even in the event of unexpected errors or&nbsp;delays.
								</p>
								<h2 class="pt-8 text-2xl font-medium lg:text-3xl">
									Acknowledgements
								</h2>
								<ul class="grid gap-x-8 md:grid-cols-2">
									<li>Jesse C., Software Engineer</li>
									<li>Josh T., Director of International Growth</li>
								</ul>
								<h3 class="pt-8 text-xl font-medium lg:text-2xl">
									Other Collaborators
								</h3>
								<ul class="grid gap-x-8 md:grid-cols-2">
									<li>Don J.</li>
									<li>Jason S.</li>
									<li>Sam T.</li>
									<li>Alejandro V.</li>
								</ul>
								<p class="pt-8 text-sm italic text-slate-600">
									ForeFlight Mobile EFB is a property of ForeFlight. All
									rights&nbsp;reserved.
								</p>
							</div>
						</SmallContainer>
					</div>
				</LargeContainer>
			</SectionContainer>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>
